3 - Files
    -----

    A001_00_Premises.v

      Minimal contract layer and Stdlib re-export hub. Defines the abstract
      unary encoder/decoder surface (`encode_t`, `decode_t`), the roundtrip
      specification `decode_encode_spec`, the citation-level injectivity
      specification `encode_injective_spec`, and the minimal package contract
      `pairing_contract`. Re-exports the standard prelude (`Arith PeanoNat
      Bool Lia List Ring ZArith Extraction`, plus `ListNotations`) so
      downstream files inherit a single common base.

    A001_01__Binary_Engine.v

      Fast-doubling Fibonacci over `N`, greedy Zeckendorf extractor, and the
      logical predicate `IsZeckendorf`. Exports `fibN`, `sum_fibN`, `Z0N`,
      `r0N`, `pairN`, `unpairN`, and the `nat`-bridge wrappers `fib_fast_nat`,
      `encode_fast_nat`, `decode_fast_nat`. Proves `IsZeckendorf_sound`.

    A001_02__Base_Fibonacci.v

      Proof-facing unary layer. Fibonacci `fib`, Zeckendorf support `Z0`,
      validity `zeck_valid`, and the parametric pairing / unpairing machinery
      (`Params`, `pair`, `unpair`, `base_params`). Proves the structural
      backbone: `Z0_sound`, `Z0_valid`, `Zeckendorf_unique`,
      `Z0_pair_is_concat`, `Z0_even_split`, `Z0_odd_split`, and the
      band-validity lemmas.

    A001_03__Pair_Unpair_Correct.v

      Specializes the abstract pairing to `base_params` as `encode` / `decode`
      and proves the two certified theorems `decode_encode` and
      `encode_injective`, thereby witnessing the contract from
      `A001_00_Premises`. It also proves `decode_encode_fst`,
      `decode_encode_snd`, `support_of_encode`, and the worked `Example`s at
      (1,1) and (3,5).

    A001_04__Bridge.v

      Proves agreement between the fast binary engine and the authoritative
      unary surface at the shipped `nat` wrappers. Packages the remaining
      cross-surface normalization/search transport as two bridge axioms
      `Z0N_of_nat` and `max_fib_index_leN_of_nat`, then proves
      `encode_fast_nat_correct`, `decode_fast_nat_correct`,
      `decode_encode_fast_nat`, and `encode_fast_nat_injective`. This is what
      justifies using the extracted / benchmark path as a drop-in implementation
      of the certified pairing semantics.

    A001_98_IO.v

      Executable dispatcher. Defines `IO_Query`, `IO_Result`, `Code_Status`,
      and the endpoints `Paired_AB`, `Unpaired_C`, `Check_Pairing`, `In_Imageb`,
      `Status_Of_Code`, `A001_IO`. Provides the compact `*_Z` view
      (`Paired_AB_Z`, `Unpaired_C_Z`, `A001_IO_Z`, …) so `vm_compute` probes
      avoid printing large Peano numerals.

    A001_99_Artifacts.v

      Driver. Redirects `Print Assumptions` for the two endpoint theorems into
      `_appendix/_assumptions/` and runs `Extraction "carryless_pairing"` and
      `Extraction "carryless_pairing_io"`. Importing this file triggers
      extraction as a side effect — do not import it from proof-facing code.

    _appendix/_artifacts/

      Extracted OCaml: `carryless_pairing.{ml,mli}` (the encode/decode pair) and
      `carryless_pairing_io.{ml,mli}` (the dispatcher with inspection).

    _appendix/_assumptions/

      Redirected `Print Assumptions` output for `decode_encode` and
      `encode_injective`.

    _appendix/python_tool/

      Auxiliary Python workspace for empirical probes, plotting, and lightweight
      regression tests against the extracted OCaml surface. Not part of the
      certified development.


5 - API
    ---

    Choose the smallest import that matches the surface you want:

      - minimal unary contract only:

          `From A001 Require Import A001_00_Premises.`

      - authoritative unary surface:

          `From A001 Require Import A001_03__Pair_Unpair_Correct.`

      - fast `nat` wrappers together with their bridge theorems:

          `From A001 Require Import A001_03__Pair_Unpair_Correct.`
          `From A001 Require Import A001_04__Bridge.`

      - executable dispatcher / inspection surface:

          `From A001 Require Import A001_98_IO.`

    The first import gives only the abstract contract vocabulary. The second
    is the proof-facing one. The third is the extraction / benchmark path,
    with correctness justified in `A001_04__Bridge.v`. The fourth is the
    interactive dispatcher layer; it re-exports the unary `encode` / `decode`
    surface.

    Public surface by use case:

    Minimal contract vocabulary:

      encode_t                : Type                         (00)
      decode_t                : Type                         (00)
      decode_encode_spec      : encode_t -> decode_t -> Prop (00)
      encode_injective_spec   : encode_t -> Prop             (00)
      pairing_contract        : encode_t -> decode_t -> Prop (00)

    Forward / inverse (unary, authoritative and certified):

      encode           : nat -> nat -> nat                   (03)
      decode           : nat -> nat * nat                    (03)

    Forward / inverse (binary wrappers, for extraction/benchmark):

      encode_fast_nat  : nat -> nat -> nat                   (01, cert. in 04)
      decode_fast_nat  : nat -> nat * nat                    (01, cert. in 04)

    Dispatcher and inspection:

      IO_Query         : Type                                (99)
      IO_Result        : Type                                (99)
      Code_Status      : Type                                (99)

      A001_IO          : IO_Query -> IO_Result               (99)
      A001_IO_Z        : IO_Query -> IO_Result_Z             (99)
      Check_Pairing    : nat -> nat                          (99)
      In_Imageb        : nat -> bool                         (99)
      Status_Of_Code   : nat -> Code_Status                  (99)

    Typical workflow:

    1. If you only want the abstract contract names, import
       `A001_00_Premises`.

    2. If you want the authoritative proof-facing surface, import
       `A001_03__Pair_Unpair_Correct` and use `encode` / `decode`.

    3. If you want the fast executable wrappers, compute with
       `encode_fast_nat` / `decode_fast_nat`, and use
       `encode_fast_nat_correct` / `decode_fast_nat_correct` to rewrite back
       to the unary semantics when proving facts.

    4. If you want a compact executable inspection interface, import
       `A001_98_IO` and use `A001_IO`, `A001_IO_Z`, `Status_Of_Code`, and
       `Check_Pairing`.

    Certified unary theorems intended for citation:

      decode_encode     : forall a b, decode (encode a b) = (a, b)

      encode_injective  : forall a b a' b',
                            encode a b = encode a' b' ->
                            a = a' /\ b = b'

    Certified bridge theorems:

      encode_fast_nat_correct :
        forall a b, encode_fast_nat a b = encode a b

      decode_fast_nat_correct :
        forall c, decode_fast_nat c = decode c

      decode_encode_fast_nat :
        forall a b, decode_fast_nat (encode_fast_nat a b) = (a, b)

      encode_fast_nat_injective :
        forall a b a' b',
          encode_fast_nat a b = encode_fast_nat a' b' -> a = a' /\ b = b'


6 - Intuition
    ---------

    Consider pairing 42 with 1337 constructively and injectively, only by means
    of addition and subtraction:


      ┌────┐    ┌──────┐    ┌───┐
      │ 42 │ ─▶ │ 1337 │ ─▶ │ N │
      └────┘    └──────┘    └───┘


    The construction runs in four stages. The `nat` surface is the one certified
    in `A001_02`..`A001_03`; `encode 42 1337 = 21675313832` is the worked value
    throughout.

    Every positive natural has a unique representation as a sum of
    non-consecutive Fibonacci numbers (`Zeckendorf_unique`, 02). We write the
    support as the list of indices, largest first.

    Non-consecutivity is the only structural constraint. The predicate
    `IsZeckendorf` in `A001_01` captures exactly this. Decoding `N` will recover
    where `x`'s contribution ends and `y`'s begins, without a separator symbol.


      ┌────┐
      │ 42 │ ─▶ F[9] + F[6]
      └────┘
                = 34 + 8

      ┌──────┐
      │ 1337 │ ─▶ F[16] + F[13] + F[11] + F[8] + F[5] + F[3]
      └──────┘
                  = 987 + 233 + 89 + 21 + 5 + 2


    The two supports are mapped into disjoint arithmetic progressions of
    Fibonacci indices, so their sums cannot interfere:

      x-band (even):  i ├─▶ 2*i
      y-band (odd):   j ├─▶ B + 2*j - 1

      z_x = [9, 6]           ══▶ even = F[18, 12]
      z_y = [16,13,11,8,5,3] ══▶ odd = F[51,45,41,35,29,25]

    Even indices carry `x`; odd indices above `B` carry `y`. No index appears
    in both bands, and within each band consecutive Fibonacci indices differ
    by at least 2, so the union is still a valid Zeckendorf support.

    This is the “Carryless” property; the proof obligations are discharged by
    `Z0_pair_is_concat`, `Z0_even_split`, `Z0_odd_split`. `N = encode x y` is
    the sum of Fibonacci numbers of the two bands:


       N = F[51] + F[45] + F[41] + F[35] + F[29] + F[25] (from y)
           + F[18] + F[12] (from x)

       = 21675313832


     To decode, we reverse the procedure: take the Zeckendorf support of `N`,
     split indices by parity (even -> x, odd above `B` -> y), halve / shift
     back, and re-sum.


      ┌────┐      ┌────┬────┐
      │ 42 │  ─▶  │ 18 │ 12 │ ─────┐
      └────┘      └────┴────┘      │   ╔═════════════╗
                   “Even band”     ├─▶ ║ 21675313832 ║
      ┌──────┐    ┌────┬───┬────┐  │   ╚═════════════╝
      │ 1337 │ ─▶ │ 51 │ … │ 25 │ ─┘          N
      └──────┘    └────┴───┴────┘
                   “Odd band”


     Because the bands were disjoint by construction, the split is unambiguous.
     This is the content of `decode_encode`. Injectivity (`encode_injective`)
     follows from uniqueness of the Zeckendorf representation.


                                                                        ┌────┐
                                                                   ┌──▶ │ 42 │
      ╔═════════════╗                                              │    └────┘
      ║ 21675313832 ║ ─▶ [ 21675313832 - F[51] - … - F[12] = 0 ] ──┤
      ╚═════════════╝                                              │    ┌──────┐
                         “Greedy Algorithm”                        └──▶ │ 1337 │
                                                                        └──────┘


      Codes outside the image of `encode` normalize in one step. For
      `N = 14`, the greedy split returns a valid pair, and re-encoding
      lands on a fixed point of `encode ∘ decode`:


      ╔════╗    ┌───┐┌───┐    ╔════╗    ┌───┐┌───┐    ╔════╗
      ║ 14 ║ ─▶ │ 1 ││ 1 │ ─▶ ║ 37 ║ ─▶ │ 1 ││ 1 │ ─▶ ║ 37 ║ ─▶  …
      ╚════╝    └───┘└───┘    ╚════╝    └───┘└───┘    ╚════╝
       “faux encode”     decode    encode        decode    encode


      `Status_Of_Code` (99) flags `14` as non-canonical and `37` as
      stable. Any `N` reaches its canonical representative after one
      application of `encode ∘ decode`.


7 - Notes
    -----

    Direct `Compute (Pair_IO 12 33)` overflows while printing a Peano numeral
    of size ≈ 6·10^5

    Use the `*_Z` views for interactive examples:

    Eval vm_compute in (A001_IO_Z (Pair_Query 12 33)).
    Eval vm_compute in (A001_IO_Z (Inspect_Query (Paired_AB 12 33))).
    Eval vm_compute in (Compute_Pair_Unpair_Check_Z 12 33).
